{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n65 \n \n \nWeek 19: July 12-18 \nOn GMT 196, ACME Flame Design test points were performed using ethylene fuel (sometimes diluted) at \na chamber pressure of 1.25 atm in about 40% oxygen (the remainder was nitrogen). Today\u2019s tests focused \nprimarily on quasi-steady flames (where the flame radius remains essentially constant in time) and \ndetermining flame temperatures near the point where soot disappears. At the end of the day, the igniter \ntip burned out. On GMT 198, Nick Hague replaced the burnt-out igniter tip. \n \nCAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG \nUpon ignition, the flame in this test point video exhibits a lot of soot, which is bright whitish-\nyellow in appearance. This extreme brightness masks the blue flame, which is the marker of the \nfuel oxidation region. With time, the soot diminishes, and the blue flame becomes more visible. \nThe harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers then slowly traverses in and out of the flame. The \nglow from the fibers allows for a good estimation of the local temperature. \n \nWeek 20: July 19-25 \nOn GMT 200, ACME Flame Design test points were performed using ethylene fuel (sometimes diluted) at \na chamber pressure of 1 atm in about 40% or 30% oxygen (the remainder was nitrogen). Today\u2019s tests \nstudied quasi-steady flames and took flame temperature measurements using the ceramic fiber array. \nThese tests were the last Flame Design tests to be performed in higher oxygen atmospheres.  \n \nOn GMT 203, Alexey Ovchinin and Christina Koch replaced the CIR Manifold #2 Bottle to support Flame \nDesign test points. \n \nWeek 21: July 26 \u2013 August 1 \nOn GMT 206, we performed the final test points for the first round of testing for ACME Flame Design. Test \npoints were performed using ethylene fuel (sometimes diluted) in a chamber environment of air (21% \noxygen, 79% nitrogen at 1 atm). The flames for these tests were short-lived and non-sooty\u2014unlike the \nflames from the tests at higher oxygen concentrations. The goal of Flame Design is to improve our \nunderstanding of soot processes so that flames can be \u201cdesigned\u201d to be soot free. \n \nTo prepare for the start of ACME s-Flame (Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion Flames), Christina \nKoch replaced the bottles on CIR Manifolds #2 and #4 on GMT 211. While monitoring the bottle pressures, \nit was noted that the pressure of the bottle at Manifold #4 was slowly decreasing. The rack was powered", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 65}}